# Transforms and Complex Methods
#
# Fourier series, Fourier transform, DFT, Laplace, and z transforms side by side: what each eats, what it turns calculus into, and how residues bring you back.

#>mathematics_transforms
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!mathematics
#:form #!comparison
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bone

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Transforms and Complex Methods"
 ~#claim: "Every transform here turns convolution into multiplication; they differ in which signals they accept and where their poles must sit for stability."
 ~#proof: "Oppenheim and Willsky give the shared table; Shannon gives sampling; Cooley and Tukey, and Heideman and colleagues, give the FFT and its history."
 ~#door: "Read the matrix row for the transform you already use, then the convolution frame, then film a fan."
 ~#audience: "engineering students between differential equations and signals or control"
 continuity: "Transforms and Complex Methods | convolution into multiplication"
 cold_open: "Every transform on this page turns convolution into multiplication; choose one by the signals it accepts."
}

